 The improper integral \( G(s) = \int_0^\infty e^{-st} \, dt \) converges if the integrand \( e^{-st} \) approaches zero as \( t \) approaches infinity. This happens when the exponent \( -st \) becomes positive as \( t \) increases, which means \( s \) must be positive. Therefore, the integral converges for \( \text{Re}(s) > 0 \).

For \( \text{Re}(s) > 0 \), the integral evaluates to:
\[ G(s) = \left[ -\frac{1}{s} e^{-st} \right]_0^\infty = \left( 0 - \left( -\frac{1}{s} \right) \right) = \frac{1}{s} \]

The Laplace transform of \( f(t) = 1 \) is given by \( L\{1\} = \frac{1}{s} \), which is valid for \( \text{Re}(s) > 0 \).

