 The resolving power of the eye is typically measured in terms of the smallest angle of separation that can be distinguished. For a 1 minute of arc, we need to convert this to a physical dimension on the retina. The human eye can resolve details on the retina that are about 1 minute of arc. Given that the average human pupil diameter is about 3.5 mm, the corresponding dimension on the retina can be approximated using the formula for angular resolution:

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} = \text{Pupil diameter} \times \text{Angular resolution} \]

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} = 3.5 \, \text{mm} \times \frac{1 \, \text{minute of arc}}{60 \, \text{minutes of arc}} \]

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} = 3.5 \, \text{mm} \times \frac{1}{60} \]

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} = \frac{3.5}{60} \, \text{mm} \]

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} \approx 0.0583 \, \text{mm} \]

Converting millimeters to micrometers (since 1 mm = 1000 μm):

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} \approx 0.0583 \, \text{mm} \times 1000 \, \text{μm/mm} \]

\[ \text{Resolution on retina} \approx 58.3 \, \text{μm} \]

This value is closest to 50 μm. Therefore, the answer is:

E. 50 microns
